JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a lightweight data format used for storing and exchanging data. It is easy for humans to read and write and easy for machines to parse and generate.

{
    "name": "Akash",
    "age": 25,
    "skills": ["React", "Redux", "JavaScript"],
    "experience": {
        "company": "Cognizant",
        "role": "Frontend Developer",
        "years": 2.3
    }
}

Key Features of JSON -----
Lightweight – Minimal syntax and easy to parse.
Readable – Uses key-value pairs, making it human-friendly.
Data Interchange Format – Used in APIs, configuration files, and web applications.
Supports Nested Structures – Can contain objects and arrays.
Language-Independent – Used in almost all programming languages.


JSON Syntax Rules
Data is stored as key-value pairs.
Keys must be strings, and values can be strings, numbers, arrays, objects, booleans, or null.
JSON data is enclosed in curly braces {}.
Arrays are enclosed in square brackets [].
No trailing commas.

Working with JSON in JavaScript
1. Convert JSON to JavaScript Object (JSON.parse()) ----
const jsonString = '{"name": "Akash", "age": 25}';
const obj = JSON.parse(jsonString);
console.log(obj.name); // Output: Akash


2. Convert JavaScript Object to JSON (JSON.stringify()) ----

const person = { name: "Akash", age: 25 };
const jsonString = JSON.stringify(person);
console.log(jsonString); // Output: '{"name":"Akash","age":25}'

Convert an Object into an Array in JavaScript -----
In JavaScript, you can convert an object into an array using methods like Object.keys(), Object.values(), and Object.entries().

1️⃣ Convert Object Keys to an Array (Object.keys()) ----
If you only need the keys of an object as an array:

const user = { name: "Akash", age: 25, role: "Developer" };
const keysArray = Object.keys(user);

console.log(keysArray);
// Output: ["name", "age", "role"]


2️⃣ Convert Object Values to an Array (Object.values()) -----
If you only need the values of an object:

const user = { name: "Akash", age: 25, role: "Developer" };
const valuesArray = Object.values(user);

console.log(valuesArray);
// Output: ["Akash", 25, "Developer"]


3️⃣ Convert Object into an Array of Key-Value Pairs (Object.entries()) ----
If you want both keys and values in an array format:

const user = { name: "Akash", age: 25, role: "Developer" };
const entriesArray = Object.entries(user);

console.log(entriesArray);

Output:
[
  ["name", "Akash"],
  ["age", 25],
  ["role", "Developer"]
]



4️⃣ Convert Object with Multiple Objects into an Array -----
If an object contains multiple objects, you can use Object.values() to get an array of objects.
const users = {
  user1: { name: "Akash", age: 25 },
  user2: { name: "Tom", age: 30 },
};

const usersArray = Object.values(users);

console.log(usersArray);

Output:
[
  { name: "Akash", age: 25 },
  { name: "Tom", age: 30 }
]
